---
title: "View Subscription"
enterprise: true
---

When a server pushes live updates, sending updates for rows the user cannot see is wasted work. The `viewSubscription` grid option tells your application which rows to subscribe to and unsubscribe from as rows enter and leave the view.

View subscription requires the `ViewSubscriptionModule` and [Row IDs](./row-ids/). This page covers the [Client-Side Row Model](./row-models/#client-side). For the Server-Side Row Model, see [SSRM View Subscription](./server-side-model-updating-view-subscription/), which works the same way and adds what changes when rows load in blocks.

The example below streams price updates for the subscribed stocks only, over a simulated websocket, and applies them with [Async Transactions](./data-update-high-frequency/).

All examples on this page use the [Quick Access Toolbar](./toolbar/) for their controls and counters.

- Scrolling produces subscribe and unsubscribe batches, logged to the console with their row count.
- Clearing **Subscription active** sets `viewSubscriptionActive` to `false`, which unsubscribes every row and stops the stream. Checking it subscribes the rows in view again.

{% gridExampleRunner title="View Subscription with Flat Data" name="flat-view-subscription" exampleHeight=615 /%}

## Handlers

Provide `subscribeRows` and `unsubscribeRows` in `viewSubscription`. When the grid is ready, it calls `subscribeRows` with the rows in view, unless `viewSubscriptionActive` is `false`. It then calls both handlers as rows enter and leave the view.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    getRowId: (params) => params.data.id,
    viewSubscription: {
        subscribeRows: (rows, params) => {
            stream.subscribe(rows.map((row) => row.id));
        },
        unsubscribeRows: (rows, params) => {
            stream.unsubscribe(rows.map((row) => row.id));
        },
    },
};
```

- `subscribeRows` receives `ViewRow` objects, which include the row `data`. Call `params.api.getRowNode(row.id)` for the row node.
- `unsubscribeRows` receives `ViewRowRef` objects, captured when the row was subscribed, as its row node may no longer exist. They hold the `id`, `type`, `parentKeys`, `level` and, for group and tree data rows, the `route`.
- Every subscribed row is unsubscribed exactly once. When rows change, `unsubscribeRows` is called before `subscribeRows`.
- The handlers are called synchronously, and a returned promise is not awaited. Queue slow work, such as sending the IDs to the server, and return straight away.
- Subscriptions are tracked by row ID. A row whose ID changes, through new row data or a transaction, is unsubscribed with its old ID and subscribed with the new one.
- New row data keeps the rows whose IDs are unchanged. A kept row stays subscribed, even when its data changes.
- A row that moves to another parent is unsubscribed with its old `parentKeys` and `route`, then subscribed with the new ones. So is a tree data row that gains its first child or loses its last one, with `hasChildren` updated.

With the Server-Side Row Model, rows are also subscribed as their blocks load and unsubscribed when a refresh replaces them. See [Loading and Refreshing](./server-side-model-updating-view-subscription/#loading-and-refreshing).

## Row Types

Each row has a `type`:

- `'group'`: a group row, created by [Row Grouping](./grouping/) or [Pivoting](./pivoting/). The `route` holds the group keys down to and including this row. `data` is `undefined`, as the grid creates group rows.
- `'tree'`: a row in [Tree Data](./tree-data/). `hasChildren` is `true` for rows with children. The `route` holds the keys down to and including this row. `data` is `undefined` for the filler rows the grid creates for missing parents.
- `'leaf'`: any other row, such as a row in flat data or a leaf row under groups. It has no `route`.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    viewSubscription: {
        subscribeRows: (rows) => {
            rows.forEach((row) => {
                if (row.type === 'group') {
                    stream.subscribeToGroup(row.route);
                } else {
                    stream.subscribe(row.id);
                }
            });
        },
        unsubscribeRows: (rows) => {
            rows.forEach((row) => {
                if (row.type === 'group') {
                    stream.unsubscribeFromGroup(row.route);
                } else {
                    stream.unsubscribe(row.id);
                }
            });
        },
    },
};
```

If the stream only has updates for leaf rows, skip group rows and tree data rows that have children in `subscribeRows`, and ignore the IDs you did not subscribe to in `unsubscribeRows`.

The Server-Side Row Model also passes group rows loaded from the server, with their `data`. See [SSRM Row Types](./server-side-model-updating-view-subscription/#row-types).

### Row Grouping {% enterpriseIcon /%} {% #row-grouping %}

The example below [groups](./grouping/) stocks by sector, and the handlers subscribe the stocks only. Each group row [aggregates](./aggregation/) the largest change and the total volume of every stock in the group, so its values fall behind while some of those stocks are out of view. See [Group Aggregates](#group-aggregates) for how to keep them current.

- Scrolling, sorting and filtering produce subscribe and unsubscribe batches.
- **New Row Data** gives every stock a new ID, so the old stocks are unsubscribed and the new ones subscribed.

{% gridExampleRunner title="View Subscription with Row Grouping" name="client-side-view-subscription" exampleHeight=615 /%}

For an example with tree data loaded in blocks, see [SSRM Tree Data](./server-side-model-updating-view-subscription/#tree-data).

## Lifecycle

The subscription starts when the grid is ready, unless `viewSubscriptionActive` is `false`.

- While the page is hidden, for example in a background browser tab or a minimised window, every row is unsubscribed. Once the page is shown again, the rows in view are subscribed. Set `pauseWhenHidden` to `false` to keep the rows subscribed while the page is hidden.
- Setting `viewSubscriptionActive` to `false` unsubscribes every row and keeps the handlers configured, for example while the grid is in an inactive tab of your application. Setting it back to `true` subscribes the rows in view. While it is `false`, nothing is subscribed, even when the page is shown.
- Setting `viewSubscription` to `undefined`, or destroying the grid, unsubscribes every row. On destroy, the rows are unsubscribed on `gridPreDestroyed`, while the API is still usable in the handlers.
- The handlers are read on each call, so new handlers take effect without unsubscribing any rows. Rows subscribed by the old handlers are unsubscribed through the new ones.

Bind `viewSubscriptionActive` to application state while the handlers stay as they are:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    viewSubscription: {
        subscribeRows: (rows) => stream.subscribe(rows.map((row) => row.id)),
        unsubscribeRows: (rows) => stream.unsubscribe(rows.map((row) => row.id)),
    },
    viewSubscriptionActive: isPricesTabSelected,
};
```

If the server loses the subscriptions, for example after a reconnect, re-send the row IDs you track, or set `viewSubscriptionActive` to `false` and back to `true` to subscribe the rows in view again.

## Excluded Rows

Only rows in view are passed to the handlers. Detail rows, full width rows, total rows (group totals, the grand total and the pivot root row) and rows from `pinnedTopRowData` or `pinnedBottomRowData` are never passed.

Rows [pinned](./row-pinning/) by the user or with `isRowPinned` stay subscribed while pinned, wherever the grid is scrolled to. A sticky group row is unsubscribed once its own position scrolls above the view, even though it stays visible at the top of the grid.

No rows are subscribed with `domLayout: 'print'`. With `'autoHeight'` every row counts as in view, so keep the normal layout for grids with many rows.

## Debouncing

The grid calls the handlers once the view has stopped moving for `debounceMs` milliseconds (default `50`), so rows scrolled past quickly are never subscribed. Raise it when each subscription is costly. Set it to `0` to subscribe while scrolling, and keep the handlers fast so scrolling doesn't lag.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    viewSubscription: {
        subscribeRows: (rows) => stream.subscribe(rows.map((row) => row.id)),
        unsubscribeRows: (rows) => stream.unsubscribe(rows.map((row) => row.id)),
        debounceMs: 150,
    },
};
```

## Group Aggregates

A group row can be in view while the rows it [aggregates](./aggregation/) are not. The handlers are only passed the rows in view, so an application that streams updates for those rows alone can show a group row whose aggregates are out of date.

Take five sector groups of 20 stocks each, with every group collapsed. Five group rows are in view, and `subscribeRows` is called with those five rows only. The grid calculates each group row from the leaf rows it holds, so all 100 stocks feed the five group rows in view.

Suppose the volume of a stock out of view changes on the server from 10 to 15. If that stock is not streamed, the grid still holds 10, and the total volume of its sector is 5 too low. With nested groups, every group above it is too low as well. Expanding the group doesn't fix the total: it was wrong while the group was collapsed, and stays wrong until the stock is updated.

Updates for the rows in view are enough when no group row in view shows an aggregate, or when the application keeps every leaf row current some other way. Otherwise, stream updates for every leaf row under each subscribed group row. The group row's `route` holds its group keys, which the server can use to find those leaf rows.

The [pivoting example](#pivoting) below does this. With pivot mode on, five sector rows are in view, so **Subscribed rows** shows 5 and **Streamed stocks** shows 100.

With the Server-Side Row Model, the server calculates the group rows and can send each one already up to date, so the client only needs the five group rows. See [SSRM Group Aggregates](./server-side-model-updating-view-subscription/#group-aggregates).

### What the Counters Measure

The examples count three different things:

- **Subscribed rows**: the rows passed to `subscribeRows` that are not yet unsubscribed. There is one subscription for each row in view.
- **Streamed stocks**: the stocks the server streams updates for because of those subscriptions. A subscribed group row streams every stock in its group.
- **Updates sent**: the number of rows the server has sent in updates so far. It keeps rising, as a streamed row is updated many times.

Every example sends all of its subscriptions and updates over one simulated websocket. Streaming 100 stocks doesn't take 100 connections: one connection carries the updates for every subscription.

## Pivoting

In [pivot mode](./pivoting/), each row in view is a group row, passed with `type: 'group'`. Each of its cells aggregates the leaf rows under it for one pivot result column. A subscription covers the whole row, including result columns scrolled out of view, so the server sends updates for everything the row aggregates.

Group rows keep their IDs when pivot mode or the pivot columns change, so only rows that move into or out of view are subscribed or unsubscribed.

The example below pivots stocks by exchange, grouped by sector, and shows the largest change and the total volume for each exchange. The grid aggregates the group rows itself, so for each subscribed group row, the handlers send its `route` and the grouped fields, and the server streams every stock in the group.

The application decides which data a subscribed row needs. Here a stream runs only when the selected columns show its data: a stock streams while its price, change or volume is visible, and the stocks of a group stream while the group row shows an aggregate. The example listens to the `columnValueChanged`, `columnVisible` and `columnPivotModeChanged` events to pause and resume the streams.

- The grid aggregates each group row from its stocks, so the five sector rows stream all 100 stocks, and **Streamed stocks** is much larger than **Subscribed rows**.
- Removing every value column in the [Columns Tool Panel](./tool-panel-columns/) drops **Streamed stocks** to 0, while the group rows stay subscribed.
- With pivot mode off, hiding **Price**, **Change %** and **Volume** does the same. Showing **Price** streams only the stocks in view, as group rows leave it blank.
- Changing the row groups or the pivot columns changes which rows are subscribed.
- **New Row Data** gives every stock a new ID.

{% gridExampleRunner title="View Subscription with Pivoting" name="pivot-view-subscription" exampleHeight=615 /%}

For the same example with the server grouping, pivoting and aggregating the stocks, see [SSRM Pivoting](./server-side-model-updating-view-subscription/#pivoting).

